import TDCSG.CriticalRadius.Five

open Verso.Genre Verso.Genre.Manual Informal

#doc (Manual) "Two disks and the critical radius" =>

%%%
tag := "disks"
%%%

This chapter specializes the compound symmetry groups of Chapter 1 to two disks in the plane,
defines the critical radius, and states the main theorem: for fivefold rotations the group is
finite exactly when the radius is below $`\sqrt{3+\varphi}`. The rest of the text proves it, in
two halves, and Chapter 8 joins them.

*Lean objects in this chapter.* The plane is the type `ℂ` of complex numbers
([`Complex`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Basic/Complex/Basic.html#Complex)),
with distance $`d(z,w)=|z-w|`. The rotation factors are values of the complex exponential
[`Complex.exp`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Analysis/Complex/Exponential.html#Complex.exp).
The square root
[`Real.sqrt`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Analysis/Real/Sqrt.html#Real.sqrt),
written `√x`, is defined on all real numbers and is $`0` for $`x\le0`. The golden ratio
$`\varphi=(1+\sqrt5)/2` is
[`Real.goldenRatio`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/NumberTheory/Real/GoldenRatio.html#Real.goldenRatio),
written `φ`. The infimum of a set of real numbers is `sInf`
([`InfSet.sInf`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/Bounds/Defs.html#InfSet.sInf));
on the real numbers it is the usual infimum of a nonempty set bounded below, and Lean gives it the
value $`0` on the empty set
([`Real.sInf_empty`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Order/Archimedean/Real/Basic.html#Real.sInf_empty)).
For a group, or any type, `G`, the statements
[`Finite G`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Basic/Finite/Defs.html#Finite)
and
[`Infinite G`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Basic/Finite/Defs.html#Infinite)
say that it has finitely or infinitely many elements; each is the negation of the other. For a
set `S`, such as an orbit, the corresponding statement is
[`S.Infinite`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Basic/Finite/Defs.html#Set.Infinite).

![Two overlapping closed disks of radius 1.5 centered at minus one and one. The left generator turns the origin clockwise through 72 degrees about minus one, to a of zero.](/figures/disks.svg)
*Figure 2.* The closed disks of radius $`1.5` about $`-1` (blue) and $`1` (orange), and one step of the left generator $`a`: it turns the origin clockwise through $`72^\circ` about $`-1`, to $`a(0)=-1+\zeta^4` with $`\zeta=\exp(2\pi i/5)`. The origin lies in both disks, so both generators move it.

Choose a point in the plane. The left generator $`a` turns it clockwise through $`360/n`
degrees about $`-1` if it belongs to the closed disk about $`-1`, including the boundary. Outside
that disk the point stays put. The right generator $`b` uses the same rule about $`1`. In the
picture $`n=5`, the radius is 1.5 and the marked point is zero. It belongs to both disks, so the
choice of generator matters.

:::definition "disk_rotation" (lean := "TDCSG.rotation, TDCSG.diskRotation, TDCSG.genA, TDCSG.genB, TDCSG.diskPerm, TDCSG.permA, TDCSG.permB")
For a center $`c`, order $`n` and radius $`r`, the disk rotation sends $`z` to
$`c+\exp(-2\pi i/n)(z-c)` if $`|z-c|\le r`, and fixes $`z` otherwise. It is a permutation of
the plane. The generators $`a` and $`b` are the disk rotations about $`-1` and $`1`.
:::

For $`n=0` Lean divides by zero with the convention $`2\pi i/0=0`, so the rotation factor is
$`\exp 0=1` and the disk rotation is the identity. This makes the definition total in $`n`; the
statements that need a genuine rotation assume $`n\ne0`.

:::definition "group_orbit" (lean := "TDCSG.rotationIsometry, TDCSG.ballRotation, TDCSG.GG, TDCSG.orbit") (uses := "disk_rotation, ball_restriction, compound_group")
The group $`GG_{n_1,n_2}(r_1,r_2)` is the compound symmetry group of two closed disks in
$`\mathbb C`: the disk of radius $`r_1` about $`-1`, carrying the cyclic group generated by the
restriction of the rotation of the plane through $`-2\pi/n_1` about $`-1`, and the disk of radius
$`r_2` about $`1`, carrying the cyclic group generated by the restriction of the rotation through
$`-2\pi/n_2` about $`1`. Write $`GG_n(r)=GG_{n,n}(r,r)`. The orbit of $`z` is the set of images
$`g(z)` for $`g` in the group.
:::

In Lean the two centers and the two radii are written `![leftCenter, rightCenter]` and
`![r₁, r₂]`, functions on the two-element index type `Fin 2`, and
`GG n₁ n₂ r₁ r₂ : Subgroup (Perm ℂ)`. The orbit `TDCSG.orbit n₁ n₂ r₁ r₂ z` is the set
`{y | ∃ g ∈ GG n₁ n₂ r₁ r₂, g z = y}`, the same set as `MulAction.orbit` of Chapter 1.

:::lemma_ "generated_by_two" (lean := "TDCSG.GG_eq_closure, TDCSG.instFGSubtypePermComplexMemSubgroupGG, TDCSG.mulAction_orbit_eq") (uses := "group_orbit, compound_generators, ball_extension_facts")
The group $`GG_{n_1,n_2}(r_1,r_2)` is the group of permutations of the plane generated by $`a`
and $`b`. In particular it is finitely generated, and the orbit of $`z` consists of the images of
$`z` under all finite words in $`a`, $`b` and their inverses.
:::

:::proof "generated_by_two" (lean := "TDCSG.ballPerm_ballRotation")
Each disk carries the cyclic group of one rotation, so by the generator lemma of Chapter 1 the
group is generated by the two extensions. The extension of the restricted rotation of a disk is the
disk rotation: on the disk both act as the rotation, and off the disk both are the identity.
:::

:::lemma_ "two_disk_support" (lean := "TDCSG.diskUnion, TDCSG.ballUnion_eq_diskUnion, TDCSG.apply_eq_of_notMem_diskUnion, TDCSG.apply_mem_diskUnion") (uses := "group_orbit, compound_support")
Every element of $`GG_{n_1,n_2}(r_1,r_2)` fixes each point outside the union of the two disks and
maps that union into itself.
:::

:::proof "two_disk_support"
The union of the two disks is the union of the balls of the compound symmetry group, so this is
the support lemma of Chapter 1.
:::

:::lemma_ "two_disk_finite" (lean := "TDCSG.finite_of_encard_orbit_le") (uses := "generated_by_two, uniform_orbits")
If every orbit of $`GG_{n_1,n_2}(r_1,r_2)` has at most $`N` points, for one natural number
$`N`, the group is finite.
:::

:::proof "two_disk_finite"
The group is finitely generated, it acts faithfully on the plane, and its orbits are uniformly
bounded, so the finiteness criterion of Chapter 1 applies.
:::

:::theorem "disjoint_disks" (lean := "TDCSG.nonempty_mulEquiv_of_add_lt_two, TDCSG.card_GG_of_add_lt_two, TDCSG.finite_GG_of_lt_one") (uses := "group_orbit, generated_by_two, compound_disjoint")
Let $`n_1,n_2\ne0` and $`0<r_1`, $`0<r_2` with $`r_1+r_2<2`. Then the two disks are disjoint
and $`GG_{n_1,n_2}(r_1,r_2)` is isomorphic to $`C_{n_1}\times C_{n_2}`, of order $`n_1n_2`. In
particular $`GG_n(r)` is finite for every $`r<1`.
:::

:::proof "disjoint_disks" (lean := "TDCSG.leftDisk_disjoint_rightDisk, TDCSG.orderOf_diskPerm, TDCSG.GG_eq_bot_of_eq_one, TDCSG.diskPerm_eq_one_of_nonpos")
The centers are at distance $`2>r_1+r_2`, so the disks are disjoint, and the product theorem of
Chapter 1 gives the product of the two cyclic groups. A disk rotation of positive radius has
order exactly $`n`. For $`r<1` and $`r>0`, $`n\ne0`, this gives a group of order $`n^2`. For
$`r\le0` each disk contains at most its center, both generators are the identity, and the group is
trivial; for $`n=0` both generators are the identity as well.
:::

:::definition "critical_radius_def" (lean := "TDCSG.infiniteRadii, TDCSG.criticalRadius") (uses := "group_orbit")
The critical radius of order $`n` is the infimum of the radii $`r` at which $`GG_n(r)` is
infinite. When the group is finite at every radius, as for $`n=1,2,3,4,6`, the infimum of the
empty set is taken to be $`0`.
:::

:::lemma_ "critical_radius_of_iff" (lean := "TDCSG.criticalRadius_eq_of_forall_finite_iff") (uses := "critical_radius_def")
If $`GG_n(r)` is finite exactly for $`r<r_0`, then the critical radius of order $`n` is $`r_0`.
:::

:::proof "critical_radius_of_iff"
The radii with an infinite group are then the closed half-line $`[r_0,\infty)`, whose infimum
is $`r_0`.
:::

:::lemma_ "infinite_of_orbit" (lean := "TDCSG.infinite_GG_of_infinite_orbit") (uses := "group_orbit")
If a point $`p` has an infinite orbit, the group is infinite.
:::

:::proof "infinite_of_orbit"
The orbit of $`p` is the image of the group under $`g\mapsto g(p)`. The image of a finite
group is finite.
:::

*The main theorem.* In the rest of the text $`n=5`, and $`R=\sqrt{3+\varphi}\approx 2.149`. The theorem is sharp: it decides
finiteness at every radius, the threshold $`R` included.

:::theorem "critical_radius_five" (lean := "TDCSG.CriticalRadius.finite_GG_five_iff, TDCSG.CriticalRadius.criticalRadius_five") (uses := "group_orbit, critical_radius_def")
For every real number $`r`, the group $`GG_5(r)` is finite if and only if $`r<\sqrt{3+\varphi}`.
Consequently the critical radius of order five is exactly $`\sqrt{3+\varphi}`, and the group is
infinite at the critical radius itself.
:::

The theorem has two halves, proved independently and joined in Chapter 8.

* *Infinite at the threshold* (Part I, Chapter 3). For every $`r\ge R`, three explicit words in
  $`a` and $`b` act on a chord through zero as an irrational rotation of a circle, so the orbit of
  zero is infinite, and with it the group.
* *Finite below the threshold* (Part II, Chapters 4 to 7). For every $`r<R`, the disk centers met
  along a word are labelled by integer vectors, words become walks in a graph on these labels, and
  the geometry of the disks puts barriers on every coordinate of the labels. The walks therefore
  stay in a box, which bounds every orbit uniformly and makes the group finite.

The radius in the picture lies below $`R`, so the group drawn there is finite.
